<DocsLayout
  title="Reactors"
  description="Turn audio into numbers and bind them to any property."
>
  <p>
    A reactor is the bridge between the audio and everything else. It analyzes
    a chosen frequency band and produces a single number between 0 and 1 on
    every frame. You then bind that number to any property on any display or
    effect.
  </p>

  <div class="placeholder">Screenshot: Reactor editor &mdash; drag a selection over the FFT to pick a frequency band</div>

  <h2>Adding a reactor</h2>
  <p>
    Click the <strong>+</strong> next to the Reactors header in the left panel.
    The new reactor appears in the list.
  </p>

  <h2>Picking a frequency range</h2>
  <p>
    Select the reactor to open the reactor editor in the bottom panel. The
    editor shows a live FFT &mdash; a bar graph of frequencies in the audio.
    Drag a selection box over the range you want the reactor to respond to.
  </p>
  <p>Rough starting points:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bass / kick</strong> &mdash; the leftmost bars.</li>
    <li><strong>Vocals / snare</strong> &mdash; the middle bars.</li>
    <li><strong>Hi-hats / cymbals</strong> &mdash; the rightmost bars.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Properties</h2>
  <h3>Output Mode</h3>
  <p>How the reactor maps the selected band onto its output:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Add</strong> &mdash; output equals the current band energy. Good for direct pulsing.</li>
    <li><strong>Subtract</strong> &mdash; output is 1 minus the band energy. Inverted pulse.</li>
    <li><strong>Forward</strong> &mdash; output accumulates upward over time driven by audio, wrapping at 1. Good for continuous motion like rotation.</li>
    <li><strong>Reverse</strong> &mdash; same as Forward but accumulates downward.</li>
    <li><strong>Cycle</strong> &mdash; bounces back and forth between 0 and 1.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Max dB</h3>
  <p>
    The loudness that maps to an output of 1. Lower this if your track is quiet
    and the reactor isn't reaching full range. Raise it if the output is
    pinned at 1.
  </p>

  <h3>Smoothing</h3>
  <p>
    Smooths the output over time. Higher smoothing means the reactor reacts
    more slowly but produces softer, less jittery motion. 0 is instantaneous.
  </p>

  <h2>Binding a reactor to a property</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Select the display or effect whose property you want to animate.</li>
    <li>In the right panel, click the small circle next to a numeric property.</li>
    <li>Pick the reactor from the list.</li>
    <li>Adjust the min/max range to control how far the property moves.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="placeholder">Screenshot: A numeric input bound to a reactor</div>

  <h2>Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Make one reactor per frequency range (bass, mid, high). Reuse them across displays.</li>
    <li>Use low smoothing on fast properties like scale, high smoothing on slow ones like color shifts.</li>
    <li>Combine Forward/Reverse modes with a rotation property for continuous spin.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
